ein biomarkers in specific  we like proteins because they are the  functional unit in the cell  moreover they tend to be more diverse  and complex  than their cousins  in the dna or rna  specifically they tend to preserve  or retain all the upstream  [Music]  genetic information  like mutations in the dna  they can also tell us about  gene expression and epigenetic  regulation of gene expression  they could also reflect processes  upstream  such as alternative splicing or  post-translational modifications  so the example we are going over today  has to do with cervical cancer  cervical cancer is  one of the most common cancers in women  worldwide i think right now it ranks as  number four  it affects about half a million women  every year  and for about half of these patients it  turned out to be fatal  as many of you know this is a type of  cancer that starts with a viral  infection specifically  with the high risk human papilloma  viruses  and over the course of a decade or so  the lesions can progress from  premalignant  into invasive carcinoma and later on  into metastatic  carcinoma  so  because of all of that  cervical cancer is an ideal  disease for screening tools as i will  show you a little later  so about 99  of all cervical cancer cases can be  linked directly to infections with high  risk hpv  and the link between a viral infection  and the cancer is that  hpv has  viral proteins specifically  e6 and e7  that bind to  and disable or degrade two tumor  suppressor genes p53 and the  retinoblastoma tumor suppressor gene  and by doing this  disables the mechanisms that are in  charge of dna damage surveillance  and  the brakes on the cell cycle in this  case  so the effects of  the virus on the tumor suppressor genes  will manifest as increased proliferation  and increase genetic instability in  these cells  with increased genetic instability  the epithelial cells in the cervix  accumulate more and more mutations  and it is a stepwise progression that  can manifest at the histopathology level  in these lesions shown here  so  the picture to your left is showing us  the  normal cervical squamous epithelium  the three  slides after that are presenting what we  call pre-cancerous or  pre-malignant lesions  they are also abbreviated as sin for  cervical  intra-epithelial neoplasm  and the reason they are numbered one two  three  they are representing increasing level  of atp  at the cell level  and at the architectural level of the  tissues  pathologists call this  these atypical features dysplasia  so while sin one will display mild  dysplasia sin 2 has moderate dysplasia  and sin 3  has  severe dysplasia  and in sin 3 the dysplasia involves all  the layers  and in fact it overlaps with another  designation that we call carcinoma in  situ  so at this level there is high potential  of progressing into invasive carcinoma  the only reason we don't call it  invasive at this point is that the  malignant cells have not broken through  the basement membrane  yet  now the last picture here to the right  is showing us an invasive carcinoma at  this level it's micro invasion  as we see a few cancer cells breaking  through the basement membrane  now the outcome from these premalignant  lesions could be very different so while  most  or the majority of sin one lesions  tend to regress in time  and only small percentage of them will  progress to send to and send through and  be beyond  however once we reach sim 3 there is a  real possibility of progression into  cancer and it is estimated to be about  10 to 20 percent  so because of this difference in  potential between sin one on one side  and sin two and three on the other side  the classification was  simplified further  by calling sin one lesion low-grade  squamous intra-epithelial lesion or  l-cell  and combining sin 2 and 3 into  high-grade lesions or itself  and  this coordinates with also  two-tier  clinical decision so while the  course of action for the low-grade  lesions is just to monitor them  the high-grade lesions require surgical  removal  in the united states approximately 13  000 cases of the invasive  carcinoma is diagnosed every year  now compare that to about one million of  the pre-cancerous lesions  and the reason behind this is the  successful application of a screening  tool called the pap test named after a  pathologist  dr papa nicola  the application for the pap test for  the last few decades has reduced  mortality from cervical cancer by some  estimates by about fourfold  and in this test the pathologist or  psychologist is looking for  a few surface cells that are shed by the  cervix of the uterus  and this is how they look like so  panel a is showing us the normal  superficial squamous epithelial cells of  the cervix  as we move on to panel b then c and d we  have all the inter-epithelial  pre-malignant lesions from sin one to  sin three  and notice  the increasing level of atypia  combined with increased nucleus to  cytoplasm ratio  reflecting perhaps the loss of  differentiation as we go through the  progression  of the pre-malignant lesions  although the pap test has been  successful as a screening tool it has  its own shortcomings  for once in  the 50 million women that undergo the  screening every year about 2 to 3  million of them  will be referred for further evaluation  or what we call ascus atypical squamous  cells of undetermined significance  moreover the pap test suffers from  inter-observer variability so it is a  subjective test  which will lead to poor sensitivity and  specificity  and just as a reminder so when we are  talking about a cancer test sensitivity  we refer to  the fact that a test that is 100 percent  sensitive will be able to diagnose all  patients with cancer  as such  while a cancer test that has poorer  sensitivity  will allow some patients to go  undetected and that's what we call false  negative  and obviously these patients now have  missed opportunities to diagnose their  cancer and treat it so they also live  with the false sense of security  while if a cancer test is not a hundred  percent  specific  it will allow  people who are  free to be diagnosed as having the  disease or what we call false positive  and that is bad in a different way that  will lead to  diagnosis of cancer in patients who do  not have the cancer  and will be followed up  probably by unnecessary  costly and invasive procedures  not to mention the emotional toll on the  patient  so then the goal of this study is to  identify  molecular markers specifically protein  markers that will help us  identify the different  stages of cervical cancer progression  so we started by collecting  10 samples from patients who have the  high grade  pre-malignant lesions and we also  collected  10 normal samples from the same patients  separately we obtained  10 biopsies from patients who have the  full-blown invasive squamous cell  carcinoma  and the experimental design of this  project was to  [Music]  take the tissue biopsies  apply laser capture micro dissection to  enrich for the phenotype of interest  then extract  the proteome from all these samples  separate all the proteins in it  into two-dimensional gel electrophoresis  combined with fluorescent probes  that will allow differential analysis  that is computer assisted and combined  with statistical methods  that will generate a list of potential  biomarkers that subsequently will be  identified using mass spectrometry and  bioinformatics  so i will walk you through all these  steps  specifically the  logic behind the experimental design  so  we all know that tumors are complex  in addition to having  malignant cells they also within the  tumor tissue you will find mesenchymal  cells like fibroblasts  endothelial cells of the blood vessels  inflammatory and immune cells  so then  we needed a way to be able to isolate  cancer cells from  the rest of  the cell types  and for that we used a technique called  laser capture micro dissection  which will allow us in real time to look  at tissue samples  and  isolate  the cancer cells by  lifting them onto a plastic cap  so here in this slide i'm showing you  real samples that we  applied laser capture micro dissection  to  so in the top panel we are looking at  the normal epithelium of the cervix  and in the first picture you see the  full tissue and in the last one you see  the one lifted onto  the cap  and similarly the bottom panel is  showing us  cancer sample  so if we focus on what we isolated on  the plastic cap we can see that we  enriched for the phenotype of interest  meaning in this case we isolated the  epithelial tissues whether they are  normal or cancerous  and we left behind  the connective tissue or straw  now if we take the tissue that was  collected on the plastic cap  and  lyse these cells to extract all the  proteins in them  we'll be able then to separate all the  thousands of proteins coming from each  cell type onto  two-dimensional gel electrophoresis  which utilizes separation by charge in  the first dimension  combined with separation by size in the  second dimension  moreover  we can utilize fluorescent probes in  what's called differential in-gel  electrophoresis or dyes  and in this case the two com compared  samples will be labeled separately with  two different fluorescent  probes  being combined together and separated on  the same gel  for instance if we are comparing  normal cervical epithelium to invasive  carcinoma  we will label each protein sample with a  different  fluorescent side eye  together separate them on the dye gel  and then we'll be able to scan for each  one of the fluorescent probes separately  then  use computer assisted analysis to gain  information about the relative abundance  of each  protein spot  in the two compared samples  so here i will walk you through the  story of one of these spots on the die  shell  this spot happened to have number 667  and what we notice about it that it  tends to have a very consistent trend  so while in the normal  epithelium it has a considerable level  of expression  that level of expression is down  regulated significantly in the  pre-malignant lesions  by about five volts  it is down regulated further as we go  into the invasive carcinoma  and the difference between the normal  tissues and the carcinoma samples was  about ten fold  and this trend was consistent amongst  all the 10 collected biopsies for each  group  so the mission then is to identify the  protein  that is in this spot  so for that first we precisely excise  the spot of interest using a robotic arm  then digest the protein inside the gel  plug using trypsin  that will generate tryptic peptides  that can be  measured using mass spectrometry  the resulting pattern is known as  peptide mass fingerprint  and using bioinformatics tools will be  able to compare or match the peptide  mass fingerprint resulting from this  protein spot  to a theoretical peptide mass  fingerprint in the database  that is  theoretically based on the genome  database  and by doing this we identify the  protein in the spot  so here is the peptide mass fingerprint  resulting from  spot number six six seven  when  used in a  protein database search  it gave a clear match on a protein named  cornell  and a similarity score was high this is  the number 220 here according to the  algorithm used in the match  scores over 64 in this case will be  significant match  the e value refers to the probability  that there is a chance there is another  protein match that has similar or  greater  similarity score  than cornell  but as you can see this number is very  very small  so at this point  this gives us confidence that there was  only one protein in the spot  and that is cornea  so at that point not much was known  about corneline except that it is a  biomarker for late epidermal  differentiation in the  stratified squamous epithelium of the  epidermis  and there was at least one study showing  decrease or loss of expression for  cornulin in esophageal cancer  so at this point we want to take a  protein marker that is identified by de  novo discovery and  transform it to where it can be used in  a routine  clinical pathology lab  and these slabs traditionally have  immunohistochemistry ability  so with the specific antibody again  against corneline we can go back and  stain  the biopsies  for corneline and the trend we observed  with the 2d gel was preserved in  immunohistochemistry  so as you can see going from left to  right the normal epithelium of the  cervix has  abundance of corneline mostly in the  upper layers of the epithelium  and there is down  regulation in the pre-malignant lesions  the ed cell  and almost  loss of expression in the invasive  carcinoma  now if we were to extend this discovery  and find out if corneline can also be  used as a prognostic marker  we went back and acquired tissue  microarray  that have  cervical cancer and pre-malignant  lesions in it and all its variation  for instance  an important histopathological parameter  is known as histopathological grade  and as we go from grade 1 to grade 3  there is loss of differentiation  which normally is associated with  increase in the aggressiveness of that  cancer  now when we stained all these tissue  microarrays for corneline we observed  that corneline tends to be  more  downregulated in the high grades of two  and three  thus that makes it a prognostic marker  in addition to being a diagnostic marker  in addition to corneal n we also  identified 22  potential markers from the 2d gel  experiment  and we envisioned that creating a panel  of the most promising markers  can  potentially augment the predictive power  of the histopathological examination  of cervical cancer tissues but also the  pre-malignant and normal tissues  interestingly enough when we did  a basic science experiment taking  keratinocytes that were immortalized by  the human papilloma virus  a lot of these  markers also  hold true  meaning they are also upregulated or  down regulated accordingly  in the immortalized cells  here i have shown you a proteomics  approach to identify novel biomarkers  for cancer  however  proteomics can be complemented nicely by  other omix techniques with the goal of  stratifying patients that  seemingly have the same disease or the  same cancer  into subgroups  we call this molecular profiling and it  could result in prognostic markers like  i showed you for cornelium for example  it could uncover  predictive markers for the sensitivity  to a certain cancer treatment or even  resistance to a treatment  they might also predict adverse events  in certain patients  so by stratifying the patients into  subgroups we can now tailor  specific  targeted therapy for each group of  patients thus maximizing  the cure rate and survival  all this is part of the emerging field  of precision medicine oncology  thank you for tuning in and if you have  any question please forward them my way

